From: Linexa Engineering <linexa@fem.linexa>
Date: 2026-08-25
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] link2symlink: resolve .l2s.* targets against the link's
 own directory; return -errno instead of raw -1

Linexa F-1/F-2 fix (audit LINEXA_TERMINAL_RUNTIME_SOURCE_AUDIT.md §5.6/§16).

## Root cause

PRoot's link2symlink extension dereferences the raw string targets of
`.l2s.*` symlinks with its OWN process cwd as the base, and leaks the bare
`ssize_t` sentinel -1 from readlink(2) into the syscall-exit plumbing, which
interprets any negative extension result as -errno. -1 is EPERM.

Concretely, `handle_sysexit_end()` (src/syscall/exit.c:770-772 poke) does:

    size = my_readlink(original, intermediate);   /* member target string */
    ...
    intermediate_proc: size = my_readlink(intermediate, final);
    /* intermediate may be RELATIVE (".l2s.unzip0001"): readlink() resolves
       it against PROOT'S CWD, not the member's directory -> ENOENT -> -1 */

and `my_readlink()` itself violates its own documented contract:

    size = readlink(symlink, value, PATH_MAX);
    if (size < 0)
            return size;      /* -1, NOT -errno */

Every guest lstat/newfstatat/rename/unlink/link over a family member whose
stored target is relative (Linexa's L2sLinkRewriter output, §1.168) or
dangling (pre-§1.168 staging targets) therefore fails with EPERM:

    dpkg: unable to stat './usr/bin/unzip': Operation not permitted

Reproduced with the verbatim termux v5.1.107.89 binary: lstat(member) and
lstat(sentinel) both return EPERM for relative targets; link() onto an
existing relative-target family returns EPERM (move_and_symlink_path's
!first_link branch has the same cwd-relative readlink); rename/unlink
"succeed" only because decrement_link_count() swallows the broken deref and
skips the count bookkeeping — leaving stale sentinel+data files behind
(dpkg's replaced family leaks, sibling members keep serving the OLD bytes
via the stale sentinel).

## The fix

Two new helpers (both in link2symlink.c, no other file touched):

  - `normalize_path()` — lexical normalization of an absolute path
    ("." dropped, ".." pops, clamped at "/").
  - `resolve_target_in_place(link_path, target)` — resolves a symlink's
    target string the way the kernel dereferences that link: a relative
    target is anchored to the directory CONTAINING the link, never to
    PRoot's cwd. Absolute targets pass through unchanged.

resolve_target_in_place() is applied at every site where a `.l2s.*` target
string is about to be used as a path:

  - handle_sysexit_end(): member branch resolves the sentinel against the
    member's dir; intermediate_proc resolves the data file against the
    sentinel's dir. `lstat(final)` failures now return -errno. A length
    guard stops the atoi() counter decode from reading out of bounds on
    malformed names (mirrors link2symlink_handle_statx's guard).
  - decrement_link_count(): both the sentinel and the data file are
    resolved before any rename/unlink/symlink mutation, so family
    bookkeeping runs on real absolute paths and recreated sentinels carry
    absolute targets (families converge to the healthy runtime shape).
    The "Skiping deref of broken link2symlink" skip semantics are
    preserved. All raw -1 returns normalized to -errno.
  - move_and_symlink_path(): the !first_link branch resolves the sentinel
    and data file the same way (fixes link() onto an existing
    relative-target family). Raw -1 returns normalized to -errno.
  - translated_path(): the member's target is resolved before the
    sentinel read, and a bare relative target (no "/") is now treated as
    its own basename instead of being skipped, so the member->data rewrite
    works uniformly for relative and absolute spellings (previously only
    absolute targets were rewritten; relative ones worked by luck of the
    kernel following the chain, diverging for O_NOFOLLOW-style opens and
    statx).

Lexical normalization is exact for link2symlink targets: PRoot only ever
stores absolute targets or bare/real-directory-relative names, and every
directory between a translated (canonical) path root and the link is a real
directory, so ".." segments climb real directories exactly like the kernel.

## Verification (x86_64 build of the same source, real ptrace, real dpkg)

Before (termux v5.1.107.89 binary):
    lstat member / sentinel over relative targets -> EPERM
    link() onto existing relative-target family   -> EPERM
    dpkg -i upgrade over relative-target family   ->
        "unable to stat './opt/l2stest/pkgbase': Operation not permitted"
    rename/unlink over member                      -> family bookkeeping
                                                     silently skipped
After (patched):
    lstat member: regular file, st_nlink = family size
    lstat sentinel: OK; stat/follow: OK; cat/execve: OK
    full family lifecycle: replace decrements siblings (nlink 3 -> 2 -> 1),
    last unlink removes sentinel+data
    dpkg -i fresh / upgrade / reinstall / remove over RELATIVE-target
    families: all succeed; no stale .l2s.* files remain after replace

## Upstream status

Not fixed in termux/proot master as of 2026-08-25 (post-.89 commits touch
PROOT_L2S_DIR descriptor hardening (7266fb3), statx nlink (894e578), and
descriptor naming (7ff389a) — none address cwd-relative target resolution
or the -1 sentinel). This patch is carried in jniLibs/proot-build/ and
compiled into the vendored libproot.so pair, consistent with the existing
0001-readlinkat-empty-pathname-fix.patch practice.

Regression test: test/test-l2s-relative-targets.c
(see jniLibs/proot-build/test-l2s-relative-targets.c)

